# JARVIS — example environment file
# ===================================================================
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED, AND NOTHING HERE COSTS MONEY.
#
# There is no API key in this file, and there is not meant to be. The brain is
# a model running on your own machine, reached through the bridge; the voice
# runs in your browser; the wake word runs in your browser. Copy this to
# .env.local only if you want to change a default.
#
# Two audiences read these variables:
#   - the BRIDGE (the Node server, bridge/server.mjs) reads the JARVIS_* ones
#     from its shell environment.
#   - the FRONTEND (the Vite app) reads the VITE_* ones from .env.local.
# Only VITE_-prefixed values ever reach the browser, so nothing sensitive
# belongs behind one.


# ===================================================================
# THE MODEL (the bridge) — the one thing worth setting
# ===================================================================
# JARVIS thinks with a local model. Any OpenAI-compatible server works:
#
#   Ollama       http://localhost:11434/v1   ollama run llama3.1
#   llama.cpp     http://localhost:8080/v1     ./llama-server -m model.gguf
#   LM Studio     http://localhost:1234/v1     (GUI — load a model, start server)
#   vLLM          http://localhost:8000/v1
#
# The defaults below are Ollama's. If your model lives somewhere else, change
# the URL; nothing else needs to move.

# Where the model server listens. Default http://localhost:11434/v1
# JARVIS_MODEL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pipeline: three models, chosen per question
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One model is a compromise — a small one answers fast and cannot use tools, a
# large one uses tools and takes seconds per sentence. Three models split that
# along the only axis that matters: what the question needs.
#
#   chat    small and fast. Conversation. This is the default.
#   vision  reads images. The only slot that can answer "what am I holding".
#   reason  the large one. Reached for when a tool has to be called correctly,
#           or when the answer needs actual thought.
#
# Which one answers is decided before anything is streamed (see pickModel in
# bridge/local-llm.mjs). An image in the conversation means vision; a question
# that names a tool or reads as technical means reason; everything else is chat.
#
# RAM, measured on the Q4_K_M quantisations these ship by default:
#
#   huihui_ai/qwen2.5-abliterate:0.5b               ~0.4 GB
#   huihui_ai/qwen2.5-vl-abliterated:3b             ~2.0 GB
#   dagbs/qwen2.5-coder-7b-instruct-abliterated     ~4.7 GB
#                                                    ------
#                                                   ~7.1 GB resident
#
# Set JARVIS_MODEL_NAME to pin every slot to one model, which is the right
# thing to do if you only have one worth running.

# The fast, conversational slot. Default huihui_ai/qwen2.5-abliterate:0.5b
# JARVIS_MODEL_CHAT=huihui_ai/qwen2.5-abliterate:0.5b

# The slot that reads images. Default huihui_ai/qwen2.5-vl-abliterated:3b
# JARVIS_MODEL_VISION=huihui_ai/qwen2.5-vl-abliterated:3b

# The slot that reasons and calls tools.
# Default dagbs/qwen2.5-coder-7b-instruct-abliterated
# JARVIS_MODEL_REASON=dagbs/qwen2.5-coder-7b-instruct-abliterated

# Pin every slot to one model. Overrides the three above.
# JARVIS_MODEL_NAME=

# Per-slot server URLs. Each defaults to JARVIS_MODEL_BASE_URL, so a big model
# can live on another machine without the others moving.
# JARVIS_MODEL_CHAT_URL=
# JARVIS_MODEL_VISION_URL=
# JARVIS_MODEL_REASON_URL=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Instruct, never base — and abliterated is not base
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A BASE model predicts the next token. It has never been taught that a function
# call is a thing it can emit, so it will DESCRIBE calling a tool instead of
# calling one, and JARVIS will appear to ignore everything he can do. No error,
# no crash — just silence where an action should have been.
#
# "Uncensored" and "base" get conflated constantly. They are not the same thing.
# Base means unaligned AND unteachable: it will not refuse you, and it will not
# understand you either. It is a starting point for fine-tuning, not a chat
# model.
#
# The defaults above are ABLITERATED INSTRUCT builds. Ablation removes the
# internal direction that produces refusals, so the model answers without
# hedging — but because the ablation is applied to an instruct build, it still
# follows instructions and still calls tools. That is what "uncensored" should
# mean here, and it is what you have been given.
#
# Ollama's default tags are the instruct builds, so these are all correct:
#
#   ollama pull huihui_ai/qwen2.5-abliterate:0.5b
#   ollama pull huihui_ai/qwen2.5-vl-abliterated:3b
#   ollama pull dagbs/qwen2.5-coder-7b-instruct-abliterated
#
# The `-base` variants are separate, explicitly-named tags, and they are the
# wrong ones here:
#
#   ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:0.5b-base      # wrong — cannot call tools


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sampling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Some servers insist on a non-empty Authorization header even with no auth.
# Local ones do not. Leave this alone unless yours complains.
# JARVIS_MODEL_API_KEY=

# Sampling temperature. Low on purpose — a spoken answer should not be
# adventurous. Default 0.6.
# JARVIS_MODEL_TEMPERATURE=0.6

# How many tool-calling rounds one question may take. A local model loops more
# than a hosted one does — it re-reads the same page, asks for the same
# screenshot twice — so this ceiling matters. Default 8.
# JARVIS_MODEL_MAX_TURNS=8

# Whole-turn timeout in ms, tools and all. Local models are slow; do not cut
# them off. Default 180000 (three minutes).
# JARVIS_MODEL_TIMEOUT_MS=180000


# ===================================================================
# BRIDGE (the server) — the rest of the JARVIS_* variables
# ===================================================================

# Port the bridge listens on (WebSocket + a few HTTP endpoints). Default 8787.
# If you change it, change VITE_BRIDGE_URL to match.
# JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# Allow effectful tools (send, tap, delete, install, pay). Off by default, so
# JARVIS can look at anything and change nothing. `npm run bridge:writes` sets
# this to 1 for you — read the decideTool gate in bridge/server.mjs first.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_WRITES=0

# Extra browser origins allowed to open the agent WebSocket, comma separated.
# Local dev origins are already allowed; add others only if you serve the app
# from a different address. Default: none.
# JARVIS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Allow clients that send no Origin header at all (curl, scripts, native apps).
# Off by default, because that is also what local malware looks like. Default 0.
# JARVIS_ALLOW_NO_ORIGIN=0

# Extra directories the bridge's /file endpoint may serve from, comma separated.
# Your home directory and the system temp directory are always included; this
# is for anything outside them, such as an external drive of footage.
# Default: none.
# JARVIS_FILE_ROOTS=


# ===================================================================
# FRONTEND (Vite) — put these in .env.local
# ===================================================================

# Where the bridge listens. Keep the port in step with JARVIS_BRIDGE_PORT above.
# Default ws://localhost:8787.
# VITE_BRIDGE_URL=ws://localhost:8787

# Browser text-to-speech engine. Default system.
#   system — the browser's built-in speechSynthesis. Instant, on-device, free.
#   kokoro — a neural voice running in the browser. Better sound, nothing
#            leaving the machine, but it downloads ~86MB of weights and
#            generates slower than realtime on most machines.
# VITE_TTS_ENGINE=system

# Which Kokoro voice, when VITE_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro. Default bm_george.
# All four are British male: bm_george, bm_fable, bm_lewis, bm_daniel.
# VITE_KOKORO_VOICE=bm_george
